{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וְהָיָה כִּי תָבוֹא אֶל הָאָרֶץ</b> (And it shall be when you come to the land). He says <b>וְהָיָה</b> in the language of joy, to signal that there is no rejoicing except in the settlement of the land, as it says (Psalms 126:2): ״אָז יִמָּלֵא שְׂחוֹק פִּינוּ״ etc." "<b>אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ נוֹתֵן לְךָ נַחֲלָה וִירִשְׁתָּהּ</b> (which HaShem your Elohim gives you as an inheritance, and you shall possess it). One must understand what the verse's formulation intends: if the subject is ״וְלָקַחְתָּ״ etc., it should have said ״תִּקַּח מֵרֵאשִׁית,״ since that is the beginning of the command. And it appears that the verse's intention is that HaShem is commanding four precepts: first — that he know in his heart that it was not by his own strength or the might of his power that he came to possess the land, but as a gift from HaShem, which is what He says: ״אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ נוֹתֵן לְךָ.״ And He was precise in saying <b>HaShem your Elohim</b> to signal that on condition that he accept His divinity upon himself, He gives. Second — to dispossess the land of its inhabitants; even though he has enough for himself in the land, nevertheless he must drive out the enemies of HaShem from His land, which is what He says: <b>וִירִשְׁתָּהּ.</b> Third — dwelling in the land, which is a precept in its own right, as we find that our Rabbis of blessed memory went to great lengths (Ketubot 111a) regarding the precept of dwelling in the land. Fourth — the bringing of first fruits, as He says: <b>וְלָקַחְתָּ</b> and so forth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:05.457919649Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}